WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.774 --> 00:00:07.254
You know, it didn’t have to be this way.&nbsp;

00:00:10.454 --> 00:00:16.294
The microcosmos is vast, and there are so&nbsp;
many organisms meeting and interacting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.294 --> 00:00:22.774
each other every day. And things often go&nbsp;
very well for the microbes we watch...they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.774 --> 00:00:30.054
often live peaceful lives that do not end with&nbsp;
them being gruesomely turned into the external&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.054 --> 00:00:34.294
stomach of a single celled parasite.
Heck, we could even be talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.294 --> 00:00:40.454
the ciliate up the screen right now, called the&nbsp;
Nassula. Only, we could have gone back to when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.454 --> 00:00:45.814
it looked more like this, the inside of its&nbsp;
body dripping with blue, yellow, and black.&nbsp;

00:00:45.814 --> 00:00:50.614
And we could have been sitting here talking about&nbsp;
how Nassula swim through clouds of plankton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.614 --> 00:00:56.294
seeking out their next meal of cyanobacteria.&nbsp;
And how, after the Nassula capture and digest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.294 --> 00:01:03.014
their prey, their cytoplasm fills with the colors&nbsp;
that once belonged to the cyanobacteria. Beads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.014 --> 00:01:09.654
of blue and blobs of yellow, and sometimes even&nbsp;
bits of pink—a colorful casualty of consumption.&nbsp;

00:01:10.774 --> 00:01:15.974
But no. We are not here today to talk&nbsp;
about cyanobacteria and pigments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.614 --> 00:01:25.414
We have other matters to discuss. Because&nbsp;
somewhere else in the microcosmos is this.&nbsp;

00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:43.494
I know, it might not look like much, just a pill&nbsp;
with a vacuole opening and closing inside it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.494 --> 00:01:50.374
while little tufts of hair move on the surface.&nbsp;
This is a suctorian, a type of ciliate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.374 --> 00:01:55.974
which makes it a relative of the Nassula.
But this is a young suctorian, a larvae.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.614 --> 00:02:02.454
Eventually, the suctorian will shed its rings of&nbsp;
cilia, and in the process, it will lose both its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.454 --> 00:02:09.974
family resemblance and its ability to swim around.
But that’s not a concern though for the suctorian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.974 --> 00:02:16.694
The ciliate that should be more worried is the&nbsp;
Nassula because a hairless suctorian doesn’t stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.694 --> 00:02:23.654
bald. It replaces its cilia with tentacles.
And this is what happens next.&nbsp;

00:02:31.494 --> 00:02:38.854
Remember, the suctorian can’t swim anymore. It&nbsp;
can’t go seek a ciliate. But it can sit around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.854 --> 00:02:43.494
with its tentacles extended, waiting&nbsp;
for the right cilia to come to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.054 --> 00:02:51.094
And when one does, the tentacles go to work,&nbsp;
sticking to and sinking into its target.&nbsp;

00:02:51.814 --> 00:02:57.654
In the area around where the suctorian’s tentacles&nbsp;
have attached, the Nassula’s cilia will stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.654 --> 00:03:04.134
beating. But it’s just a small portion of the&nbsp;
cilia for now, not enough to stop it entirely. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.134 --> 00:03:10.614
so for a time, the Nassula will continue swimming,&nbsp;
seemingly unaware of the suctorian on its back.&nbsp;

00:03:11.334 --> 00:03:17.894
For the suctorian, this is a good deal. It doesn’t&nbsp;
need to swim anymore, it has Nassula to do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.014 --> 00:03:22.614
But the Nassula is more than just&nbsp;
a free ride around the microcosmos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.254 --> 00:03:31.414
It is also the suctorian’s next meal.&nbsp;

00:03:36.374 --> 00:03:41.334
Sometimes, we are what we eat. And&nbsp;
sometimes, we are what’s eating us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.894 --> 00:03:48.454
And with the suctorian attached, the Nassula&nbsp;
becomes more and more difficult to recognize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.454 --> 00:03:54.134
looking more and more like a whale with&nbsp;
outsized barnacles protruding from its side.&nbsp;

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:59.654
Near the site where they’ve attached to the&nbsp;
Nassula, the suctorian will begin to digest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.654 --> 00:04:07.654
bits of their host. This means that the Nassula&nbsp;
now has three uses for the suctorian: a free ride,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.654 --> 00:04:13.414
a meal, and now, a temporary external stomach.&nbsp;

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:20.134
Like the cilia that stopped beating, this&nbsp;
digestion is localized, allowing the Nassula’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.134 --> 00:04:27.894
organelles to contract and the organism to live&nbsp;
as it is slowly being digested from within.&nbsp;

00:04:27.894 --> 00:04:33.734
The suctorian doesn’t have an oral cavity&nbsp;
to act as a mouth, but it doesn’t need one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.734 --> 00:04:40.294
It has tentacles, which shorten in length and&nbsp;
thicken in width as the suctorian begins to pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.294 --> 00:04:45.894
grains of the digested ciliate through&nbsp;
it. And the Nassula, once kaleidoscopic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.894 --> 00:04:55.414
with the remains of its own meals, now passes&nbsp;
those colors on to its unwelcome passenger.&nbsp;

00:04:56.934 --> 00:05:02.614
For some suctorians, one host is not enough to&nbsp;
satiate its appetite. If you look at the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.614 --> 00:05:08.934
corner of the Nassula here, you can see that one&nbsp;
of the suctoria has its tentacle poking outwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.934 --> 00:05:14.694
keeping them extended and ready in case&nbsp;
another Nassula comes along to add to its meal.&nbsp;

00:05:17.494 --> 00:05:24.214
And this is not a matter of moving from&nbsp;
one host to the next. No, the suctorian is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.214 --> 00:05:32.294
happy to attach itself to multiple Nassula,&nbsp;
assembling a crowd of hosts and parasites.&nbsp;

00:05:33.254 --> 00:05:41.814
But the suctorian has one more use left for&nbsp;
its host: a nest. Not a real nest, of course.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.814 --> 00:05:48.854
Suctorians don’t lay eggs. Instead, they bud,&nbsp;
growing their young out of themselves. And they do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.854 --> 00:05:55.654
this while attached to their host, sprouting their&nbsp;
young on the outside of their own nonmotile bodies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.654 --> 00:06:02.934
until the new larvae is ready for life on its own.
The baby suctorian is equipped with its own cilia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.934 --> 00:06:09.174
able to swim away for its own independent&nbsp;
life. But at the end of the day, suctorians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.174 --> 00:06:15.094
are parasites. They’re not meant to be on&nbsp;
their own. So one day, when they’re ready,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.094 --> 00:06:22.134
those larvae will follow the path of their parent,&nbsp;
settling down for a life on their own meal.&nbsp;

00:06:24.294 --> 00:06:28.774
It didn’t have to be this way, of course.&nbsp;
The Nassula could have lived its whole life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.774 --> 00:06:34.454
without ever having come across a suctorian&nbsp;
parasite. It could have spent its life contentedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.454 --> 00:06:40.774
grazing upon cyanobacteria and converting itself&nbsp;
into a multicolored monument to its own meals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.494 --> 00:06:47.174
And many Nassula have lived such lives,&nbsp;
meeting their ends in other ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.174 --> 00:06:54.214
perhaps more dramatic or perhaps less.
But our world is maintained by these meetings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.854 --> 00:07:00.134
by these pigments, these chemicals, this&nbsp;
energy, passing from one organism to the next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.774 --> 00:07:05.654
and by the fateful contact of some&nbsp;
patient, outstretched tentacles.&nbsp;

00:07:12.134 --> 00:07:18.054
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

